<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nikolai Gogol</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1809">1809</a>–<a href="/years/1852">1852</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Nikolai Vasilyevich Gogol, Gogol-Ianovskii</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nikolai Gogol was a Russian novelist, short-story writer and playwright of Ukrainian origin, born in Sorochyntsi in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Poltava</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>. His early stories, Evenings on a Farm Near Dikanka (1831–1832), drew on Ukrainian folklore, while The Government Inspector and Dead Souls satirised corruption in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. He used the grotesque in stories such as &quot;The Nose&quot; and &quot;The Overcoat&quot;, and from 1836 to 1848 lived mostly abroad, above all in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Deeply depressed, he burned most of the second part of Dead Souls on the night of 24 February 1852 and died nine days later.</p></div>
